Sometimes we encounter data with high variation, an approach to solve such problem is to divide them into categories/bins and then encoding each category(using a number).
There are two techniques to turn numerical data into categorical data.
    - Discretization(Binning)
    - Binarization

Discretization(binning):
    It is a process of transforming continuous variables into discrete variables by creating a set of contiguous intervals that span the range of the variable's values. Discretization is lso called binning, where bin is an alternative name for interval

    Why use it:
        1. To handle outliers
        2. To improve the value spread
    
    Types of discretization
        - Unsupervised
            -> Equal width(uniform) binning 
                = Define the no of bins
                = Define width of bins (max - min / no of bins)
                = Calculate frequency
                = Plot them in histogram
                * Works well for outlier, no affect on uniform spread.

            -> Equal frequency(quantile) binning
                = Define no of bins
                = If there are n bins then each bins contains nth quantile value(i.e. if 10 bins then our fiirst bin would contain data that reaches till the 10th quantile value - 1st bin have 10 quantile, 2 bin 20th quantile and so on.)
                = Therefore the width of each bin won't be equal.
                * Works well for outlier, also ensure uniform value spread(normalize / balance them), therefore more commonly used.
                
            -> K-Means binning
                = In it we use clustering algorithm(K-means), even in 2d,3d,...10d...nd.
                = Used when data is somewhat discrete and spreaded in clusters.
                = Intervals in this algo are termed as centroid.
                - Does clustering based on calculating distance b/w data points.
         Code:
             = use sklearn class : KBinsDiscretizer and pass parameters(bins= ,strategy = uniform/quantile/kmeans,encoding = ordinal/ohe)      

        - Supervised 
            -> Decision tree binning

        - Custom Binning
            = CLustering based on your personal(doomain knowledge) preference(like dividng ages into child, young, adult).
            = sklearn don't support it, use pandas for it.
        

